## 3. The evidence — realified count loses the off-axis information

From `probe6.log`, the effective exponents at `eps→0` the two paths hand the IBP reducer:

**Unlifted** (Direct mode keeps exponents complex):
```
sector 18:  {0, 1/4 + I/2, 1/2 + I, 3/4 + I/2, 5}     <- ONE bare 0 (the pole); the rest Re>0
sector 23:  {3/2 + I, 0, 1/4 + I/2, 1/2 + I, 5}
```
Exactly **one** genuine pole direction (`α0 = 0`, Re=0 ∧ Im=0). Every other direction has `Re>0`
(carrying `Im≠0`), so it is convergent. IBP sees one pole → **succeeds**.

**Lifted + `SplitRealImag`** (the imaginary parts have already been split off into the phase, so the
exponents the guard sees are **real**):
```
sector 22:  {10, 4/13, 3/4, 0, 1/2}    <- reducer finds ONE divergent variable y_4 (the bare 0)
sector 23:  {10, 3/4, 0, 1/4, 1/2}
```
`IBPReduceSector` correctly finds `nDiv = 1` and runs its single IBP step (`1 -> 31 terms`). It then
`$Failed` **inside `IBPProcessSector`** — the post-IBP guard "verify all `α0 > 0`"
(`tropical_eval.wl:5148–5157`, the `ERROR: … alpha0[i] = … <= 0` at `:5152`) trips on a direction
whose **realified** exponent has `Re ≤ 0`. Because `SplitRealImag` peeled the imaginary part into the
oscillatory phase, this guard sees only `Re` and cannot distinguish a genuine soft direction
(`Re=0 ∧ Im=0`) from an **off-axis** one (`Re=0, Im≠0`, value `1/(iθ)` finite). It reports
"nested / higher-order pole" where — per the physics of §1.1 — **there is only one true pole.**

This is the *same* full-complex-exponent principle `planIBPCX` already applied to the prefactor
(`ibpImagPole` `:4950`; driver off-axis branch `:6199–6241`; `splitdivmono` message `:216`), simply
**not yet applied to the count / residual-divergence guard.**

