## 4. Two candidate root causes — and the diagnostic that distinguishes them

The reducer reported `nDiv=1` at the *start*; the failure is *post*-IBP. So there are two
possibilities, needing **different** features:

- **(A) Off-axis miscount (most likely).** A post-IBP term has a direction with `Re(α0)=0` but
  `Im(α0)≠0`. It is finite (off-axis), but the `Re`-only `verify α0>0` guard counts it as a residual
  divergence. **Feature:** extend the off-axis criterion (already in `ibpImagPole`) to the
  count/verify guard — a direction is a genuine pole only if `Re(α0)=0` **and** `Im(α0)=0`; off-axis
  directions are IBP-reduced-to-finite, not counted. Then `one real pole + N off-axis ⇒ nDiv=1`.

- **(B) Genuine per-cone higher-order residual.** The lift's change of variables produces an
  individual cone with a real `1/eps^2`-type structure (which must cancel in the cone sum, since the
  physical total is a simple pole). **Feature:** iterated IBP (reduce the residual soft direction in a
  second step), i.e. lift the "single divergent variable per sector" restriction
  (`nestedIBP`, `:201`, `:5010–5013`). This is strictly more work than (A).

**Discriminating diagnostic (cheap, read-only, ~1 cone):** carry the *full complex* effective
exponents through the IBP step on lifted `Fourptpp` presector-1 / sector-22 and inspect the direction
that trips `:5148–5157`. If `Im≠0` → case (A). If `Im=0, Re<0` → case (B). I can run this on request;
the physics (single simple pole) makes (A) the expected outcome, but I have **not** confirmed it —
stated honestly so the engine work targets the right feature.

